1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,570
Léa: Hello everyone,

2
00:00:01,570 --> 00:00:03,540
welcome to The Cozy French,

3
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:06,360
your little get-together for listening to French,

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:10,240
discovering everyday expressions and practising with us.

5
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:11,360
I'm Léa.

6
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:12,720
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu.

7
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:14,160
Whether you're at home,

8
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,480
on public transport or taking a little break,

9
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:19,440
we're very happy to be with you again.

10
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:21,480
Léa: Make yourselves comfortable.

11
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:25,320
Today, we're going to talk about something that concerns us all:

12
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:27,410
concentration.

13
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:30,530
How to keep your attention on what you've chosen

14
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:33,800
to do when so many things pull us elsewhere.

15
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:39,280
Mathieu: A message, a noise, an idea that comes at the wrong moment.

16
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:42,460
Sometimes, a few seconds are enough to forget

17
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:44,400
what you were doing.

18
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:48,960
Léa: This conversation is for people at level A2 to B1.

19
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:51,920
We'll explain useful expressions along the way

20
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:56,490
with examples and a few chances for you to speak in turn.

21
00:00:56,540 --> 00:00:59,600
Mathieu: And you don't need to understand every word.

22
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,200
First, just try to follow our conversation,

23
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:04,600
as if you were sitting with us.

24
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,080
Léa: By the way, Mathieu, before we started,

25
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:09,200
I asked you if you were ready.

26
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:11,930
You said yes, absolutely.

27
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:13,960
But you were looking somewhere else.

28
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:17,960
Mathieu: I was watching a fly trying to get out through a closed window.

29
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:22,530
Léa: So you were focused, just on another guest.

30
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:25,020
Mathieu: She had a complicated plan.

31
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:27,080
I was following her journey.

32
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:30,240
Léa: To start, think of the last time you

33
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:33,760
wanted to do something, before finding yourself

34
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:36,120
doing something completely different.

35
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:40,720
Mathieu: I wanted to free up a bit of space on my computer.

36
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:43,080
I found an old class photo.

37
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,600
An hour later, I was looking up the name of

38
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:47,760
my old PE teacher.

39
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:50,030
Léa: And did you free up some space?

40
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,520
Mathieu: I deleted two blurry photos.

41
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:54,760
An excellent hour of work.

42
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:56,880
Léa: I have a fairly similar story.

43
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,160
On Saturday, I wanted to make a photo album

44
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:01,000
for my mother's birthday.

45
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,360
Mathieu: That's a lovely idea.

46
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:03,040
Léa: Yes.

47
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:05,760
I had the photos, I'd chosen the website,

48
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:08,680
I'd even made a cup of coffee.

49
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:10,030
Two hours later,

50
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:12,800
I still hadn't made a single page.

51
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:14,120
Mathieu: What happened?

52
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,240
Léa: I started by sorting the photos.

53
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:18,970
Then I renamed the folders.

54
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:22,840
Then I wanted to find the exact date of a trip.

55
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:25,920
And that's when I opened my old messages.

56
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:29,960
Mathieu: You'd started an album and you opened a museum.

57
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:32,400
Léa: With very detailed archives.

58
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,240
At one point, I reread a conversation about a

59
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:38,280
restaurant booking from 2019.

60
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:39,720
Mathieu: Was it good, at least?

61
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:43,520
Léa: According to my messages, the desserts were excellent.

62
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,730
But my mother was still waiting for her album.

63
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:49,300
Mathieu: What's tricky is that all these little

64
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,120
actions had something to do with your project.

65
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:54,640
Léa: Exactly, I wasn't watching a TV series.

66
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:57,640
I really felt like I was making progress.

67
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:01,320
And yet I kept putting off choosing the photos.

68
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:04,440
Mathieu: Because choosing was the hard part?

69
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:05,040
Léa: Yes.

70
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:08,220
I was afraid of forgetting someone or choosing a photo

71
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:09,840
my mother wouldn't like.

72
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:13,890
Mathieu: You can say: I'm putting this decision off until later.

73
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:17,720
To put off something means deciding to do it

74
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:20,600
later instead of doing it now.

75
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:24,400
Léa: And sometimes I don't even make that decision clearly.

76
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:27,640
I just find another little thing to do.

77
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:30,810
Mathieu: Like cleaning your desk at the exact moment

78
00:03:30,810 --> 00:03:33,000
you have to fill in a form.

79
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,540
Léa: Yes, my desk is never as clean as when

80
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:39,080
I have a complicated form to fill in.

81
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:40,880
Mathieu: We often talk about phones,

82
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:43,730
but it's not my only distraction.

83
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:46,790
For me, there are also noises,

84
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:49,480
ideas, and sometimes hunger.

85
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:51,440
Léa: Do you work well when you're hungry?

86
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:54,290
Mathieu: No, I start reading every word as if

87
00:03:54,290 --> 00:03:56,640
it were the name of a dish.

88
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:59,040
Léa: A meeting turns into a gratin?

89
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:01,390
Mathieu: Depends on the meeting.

90
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:04,680
But some would be better off as gratins.

91
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:08,460
Léa: A distraction is something that takes our attention away

92
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:09,600
from what we're doing.

93
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:11,440
A noise can distract me.

94
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:12,850
So can a worry.

95
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:14,520
Mathieu: And to talk about yourself,

96
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:19,000
you can say "I get distracted easily."

97
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,280
Léa: Or "I find it hard to concentrate when there's noise."

98
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:28,280
To have trouble doing something

99
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:31,680
means finding it difficult.

100
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:35,040
Mathieu: I find it hard to read when the TV is on.

101
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:37,200
Even if I'm not watching the show,

102
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:41,250
I listen to people talking.

103
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:45,480
Léa: For me, it's mostly songs with lyrics.

104
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:49,080
I start writing a work message and,

105
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:52,800
suddenly, I want to add the chorus.

106
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:54,380
Mathieu: Dear Madam,

107
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:59,080
Regarding our appointment, I will always love you.

108
00:04:59,080 --> 00:04:59,930
Léa: Exactly.

109
00:04:59,930 --> 00:05:03,600
Very warm, but not very appropriate.

110
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:05,180
Your turn now.

111
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:07,040
Mathieu: Finish this sentence.

112
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:10,690
I find it hard to concentrate when…

113
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:13,600
Léa: It could be when I'm tired,

114
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:18,120
when someone talks to me or when my phone rings.

115
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:22,320
Choose a situation that sounds like you.

116
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:25,630
But there's one moment when my distractions

117
00:05:25,630 --> 00:05:28,080
bother me in particular:

118
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:30,400
when I'm with someone.

119
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:33,920
Mathieu: When you look at your phone during a conversation?

120
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:34,800
Léa: Sometimes.

121
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:38,500
But even without a phone, I can be thinking about my

122
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:42,040
answer instead of listening to the end of the sentence.

123
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:43,720
Mathieu: I know that feeling.

124
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:47,000
Someone tells me about their holiday and I'm already preparing

125
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,120
my own holiday story.

126
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:53,200
Meanwhile, I miss part of theirs.

127
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:55,290
Léa: The other day, a friend was telling me

128
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:57,680
about a change at her work.

129
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:59,650
I was thinking about my train,

130
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:01,680
my bag, the time.

131
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:04,890
I answered, "Oh, great!"

132
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:07,360
Mathieu: And it wasn't great?

133
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:11,320
Léa: She'd just said her favourite colleague was leaving.

134
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:15,680
So I had to admit I hadn't really been listening.

135
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:17,400
Mathieu: What did you say to her?

136
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,720
Léa: Sorry, my mind was elsewhere.

137
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:22,480
Can you tell me again what happened?

138
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:25,240
To have your mind elsewhere.

139
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:29,800
Mathieu: It means thinking about something other than what's happening now.

140
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,890
It's a very common expression.

141
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:34,840
Léa: And my friend started again.

142
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,960
It was a bit awkward for a few seconds.

143
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:43,320
But afterwards, our conversation was much nicer.

144
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:46,320
Mathieu: It's useful when you're learning French, too.

145
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:49,920
You can say, "Sorry, I lost the thread.

146
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:51,920
Can you go back?"

147
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:53,230
Léa: To lose the thread

148
00:06:53,230 --> 00:06:57,760
means no longer following a conversation or an explanation.

149
00:06:57,760 --> 00:07:00,850
You're not quite sure where you are anymore.

150
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:02,960
Mathieu: Can you give us an example?

151
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:07,200
Léa: Sure. You're telling me how you fixed your bike.

152
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:08,840
Someone calls me.

153
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:12,400
When I come back, I say, "I lost the thread,

154
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:14,200
were you talking about the wheel?"

155
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:16,500
Mathieu: And I answer, "Yes,

156
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:19,360
but now we're talking about the brake.

157
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:22,120
A great mechanical adventure."

158
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:23,600
For your album,

159
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:26,270
what helped you in the end?

160
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:30,920
Léa: I closed the old messages and asked myself a question.

161
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:33,110
What do I want to have finished

162
00:07:33,110 --> 00:07:36,080
before I close my computer?

163
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:38,280
The whole album?

164
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:39,880
At first, yes.

165
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,920
Then I looked at the time

166
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:45,200
and chose only the first page.

167
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:48,680
A photo of my mother and a short text.

168
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:51,810
Mathieu: Then you knew what you were looking for.

169
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:56,240
Léa: Exactly, I could look at a photo and ask myself,

170
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:58,640
"Does it work for this page?"

171
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:01,080
That helped me decide.

172
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:04,430
Mathieu: I do something similar before

173
00:08:04,430 --> 00:08:06,760
cooking a slightly long meal.

174
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:08,810
I read the whole recipe,

175
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:12,800
then I start with one specific action.

176
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:14,880
Léa: Like chopping the vegetables?

177
00:08:14,880 --> 00:08:15,800
Mathieu: Yes!

178
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:18,610
Otherwise, I take out a pan,

179
00:08:18,660 --> 00:08:20,860
I think about the sauce,

180
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:26,920
I look for a spice, and the onions are still waiting.

181
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:29,600
Léa: They're polite, those onions.

182
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:30,800
Mathieu: Very!

183
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:34,200
But then they make me cry.

184
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:36,120
Léa: To talk about this choice,

185
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:38,400
you can say, "For now,

186
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:39,800
I'm dealing with this."

187
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:44,680
It means, "This is the activity that matters right now."

188
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:48,370
Mathieu: For now, I'm working on the introduction.

189
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:51,640
I'll fix the layout afterwards.

190
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:55,720
Léa: Or, "Today, I want to understand this dialogue,

191
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:59,280
I'll look up the new words afterwards."

192
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:03,600
Mathieu: I like this permission to leave some things for later.

193
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:07,280
Otherwise, I try to do everything at once.

194
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:11,000
Léa: And when an idea comes to you while you're working?

195
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:12,960
Mathieu: It depends.

196
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:16,010
If I remember I need to buy bread,

197
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:19,920
I might want to place an order right away,

198
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:23,000
then I look at the other products.

199
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:26,480
Léa: And your document stays open behind the yoghurts.

200
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:27,970
Mathieu: Exactly!

201
00:09:27,970 --> 00:09:30,080
So now, when I can,

202
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:33,440
I just write "Bread" on a sheet of paper next to me.

203
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:35,080
Léa: To come back to it later?

204
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:39,600
Mathieu: Yes, I know I won't necessarily deal with it right away,

205
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:43,810
but I don't need to keep that idea in my head.

206
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:47,280
Léa: I do that when I'm writing an important message.

207
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:50,640
If I think of someone else to contact,

208
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:55,120
I write down their name without opening a new chat.

209
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:58,000
Mathieu: Of course, if something is urgent,

210
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,240
you deal with it.

211
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:02,280
The sheet of paper doesn't decide for us.

212
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:07,720
Léa: Luckily, I wouldn't like to work for such a bossy sheet of paper.

213
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:10,800
Mathieu: We could write "To look at later."

214
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,120
Léa: It's simple and useful.

215
00:10:13,170 --> 00:10:16,760
Mathieu: "To look at later": booking, bill,

216
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:18,080
Paul's present.

217
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:20,890
Léa: And what if Paul's present becomes urgent because

218
00:10:20,890 --> 00:10:22,920
his birthday is tonight?

219
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:25,840
Mathieu: Then Paul comes before the sheet of paper.

220
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:29,200
And I pick a bookshop near his place.

221
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:32,310
Léa: Sometimes, the distraction comes from other people,

222
00:10:32,310 --> 00:10:34,560
even when they're very kind.

223
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:37,480
Mathieu: Yes, in a shared flat,

224
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:41,970
someone might ask a little question every 5 minutes.

225
00:10:42,020 --> 00:10:44,640
Léa: And each question seems reasonable,

226
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:48,440
but in the end, you can't get back to what you were doing.

227
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:50,640
Mathieu: You can explain what you need.

228
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:53,600
For example: I'm finishing something.

229
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:56,040
Can we talk about it in a quarter of an hour?

230
00:10:56,040 --> 00:10:58,680
Léa: A quarter of an hour is 15 minutes.

231
00:10:58,680 --> 00:11:03,320
And naming a specific time can make the request clearer.

232
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:04,400
Shall we try?

233
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:07,560
Mathieu: Do you want to ask my opinion on something?

234
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:10,850
Léa: Mathieu, do you prefer the photo where I'm by

235
00:11:10,850 --> 00:11:13,840
the lake or the one in front of the mountain?

236
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:17,960
Mathieu: I'd like to look, but I'm finishing this message.

237
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:19,960
Can you give me 10 minutes?

238
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:22,440
Léa: OK, I'll come back later.

239
00:11:22,440 --> 00:11:24,040
Mathieu: And 10 minutes later,

240
00:11:24,040 --> 00:11:26,360
I really do look at the photos.

241
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:31,840
Otherwise, my request ends up looking like a way to avoid you.

242
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:33,490
Léa: Yes, and on my side,

243
00:11:33,490 --> 00:11:36,640
if it's urgent, I can say so.

244
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:39,130
I need your answer now.

245
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:41,840
The order closes in 2 minutes.

246
00:11:41,840 --> 00:11:44,720
Mathieu: Now I understand the situation better.

247
00:11:44,720 --> 00:11:46,560
We can decide together.

248
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:48,240
Léa: Try repeating.

249
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:50,040
I'm finishing something.

250
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:52,400
Can we talk about it in 10 minutes?

251
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:57,280
Mathieu: One calm sentence can already help protect a little moment.

252
00:11:57,280 --> 00:12:00,320
And we're allowed to stop, too.

253
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:02,480
I'm saying this because sometimes

254
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:07,410
I stay in front of my screen just to look busy.

255
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:09,720
Léa: You read the same line several times?

256
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:12,280
Mathieu: Yes, by the third reading,

257
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:13,760
I know the words.

258
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:17,160
By the fourth, I start to recognise the font

259
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:19,320
personally.

260
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:21,680
Léa: When that happens to me, I like to get up,

261
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:24,840
drink some water or go out for a few minutes.

262
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:27,520
Depending on the day, it does me good.

263
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:30,480
Mathieu: I like to move my shoulders a bit.

264
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:34,850
I often find I've been sitting like a shrimp.

265
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:37,480
Léa: A very professional shrimp.

266
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:41,840
Mathieu: With an email address and lots of responsibilities.

267
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:46,040
Léa: To announce a break, you say, "I'm taking a little break."

268
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:48,200
And when you come back: "Right,

269
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:49,440
back to it."

270
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:51,600
Mathieu: To get back to something

271
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:54,600
means starting again after a stop.

272
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:59,760
"I'm getting back to work" or "I'm getting back to reading."

273
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:03,770
Léa: You may also need a proper end to the day.

274
00:13:03,820 --> 00:13:07,920
If I'm exhausted, staying a long time in front of my

275
00:13:07,920 --> 00:13:10,240
project doesn't necessarily help.

276
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:13,760
Mathieu: There are days when I can change my plans.

277
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:16,520
Others when I have an obligation.

278
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:19,660
In that case, I look at what's really

279
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:22,800
necessary and what can wait.

280
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:26,920
Léa: And a hobby can perfectly well take all our attention.

281
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:30,440
I don't need to turn every quiet moment

282
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:32,290
into a work session.

283
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:35,350
Mathieu: Luckily, I like listening to music

284
00:13:35,350 --> 00:13:38,440
without having to write a report.

285
00:13:38,440 --> 00:13:40,840
Léa: Speaking of which, let's talk about your listening.

286
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:43,120
If you're cooking right now,

287
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:47,200
maybe you followed the story while chopping your vegetables.

288
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:50,640
Mathieu: And maybe you missed an expression.

289
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:54,520
That happens in your native language, too.

290
00:13:54,520 --> 00:13:59,330
Léa: You can come back later to a short passage that interests you.

291
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:03,400
For example, our request for 10 minutes of quiet.

292
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:08,960
Mathieu: Listen to the question, then try to answer before me.

293
00:14:08,960 --> 00:14:13,120
It gives your listening a small, concrete goal.

294
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:15,160
Léa: Let's try it now.

295
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:18,360
Mathieu, what distracts you when you read?

296
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:21,200
Mathieu: Conversations around me.

297
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:24,240
I find it hard to read when several people are talking.

298
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:25,970
Léa: And what do you do?

299
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:29,480
Mathieu: If I can, I look for a quieter spot.

300
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:33,840
Otherwise, I save my book for another time.

301
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:34,680
Léa: Your turn!

302
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:38,160
What distracts you during an activity you enjoy?

303
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:41,040
Mathieu: And what do you do in that situation?

304
00:14:41,040 --> 00:14:43,520
Léa: You can link the two answers.

305
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:47,480
Noise distracts me, so I close the door.

306
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:52,690
Here, the word "alors" introduces what you do as a result.

307
00:14:52,740 --> 00:14:54,400
Mathieu: I think about my shopping,

308
00:14:54,400 --> 00:14:57,040
so I write it down for later.

309
00:14:57,040 --> 00:15:01,560
Léa: Or: I'm tired, so I take a break.

310
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:05,160
Mathieu: Three little sentences, three different situations.

311
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:07,880
No need for a complicated explanation.

312
00:15:07,880 --> 00:15:10,080
Léa: There's one important moment left:

313
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:14,600
the one when you realise you've left your activity again.

314
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:19,280
Mathieu: My first reaction is sometimes to look at the time

315
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:22,130
and get angry with myself.

316
00:15:22,180 --> 00:15:23,160
Léa: Does that help?

317
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:24,160
Mathieu: Not much.

318
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:27,960
I spend time commenting on what I haven't done.

319
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:32,120
Léa: For my album, I simply asked myself where I was.

320
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:34,640
The first page was started,

321
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:38,240
I reread the short text and added the date.

322
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:41,120
Mathieu: So you found something you could do?

323
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:43,160
Léa: Yes, and if I'd forgotten,

324
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:46,120
I could have left a note before my break.

325
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:50,330
Next step: choosing the cover photo.

326
00:15:50,380 --> 00:15:53,040
Mathieu: I'd like to do that with my documents.

327
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:55,040
When I reopen them the next day,

328
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:59,400
I sometimes have to investigate my own intentions.

329
00:15:59,400 --> 00:16:03,600
Léa: Why did I write only the word "umbrella" in the middle

330
00:16:03,600 --> 00:16:04,920
of this text?

331
00:16:04,920 --> 00:16:09,960
Mathieu: Exactly, and the author is never available to answer.

332
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:12,160
Léa: Another useful expression.

333
00:16:12,160 --> 00:16:13,720
Where was I?

334
00:16:13,720 --> 00:16:17,240
You say it when you're looking for the point where you stopped.

335
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:19,210
Mathieu: Where was I?

336
00:16:19,260 --> 00:16:22,360
Ah yes, I had to finish this paragraph.

337
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:26,920
Léa: For a conversation too: where were you in your story?

338
00:16:26,920 --> 00:16:28,600
Mathieu: At the fly, I think.

339
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:32,640
She finally got out when I opened the other window.

340
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:36,520
Léa: At least one guest finished her project today.

341
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:42,280
Mathieu: To finish, we suggest you tell a distraction story in three steps.

342
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:44,080
What you wanted to do,

343
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:47,970
what happened, and how you got back to it.

344
00:16:48,020 --> 00:16:49,680
Léa: Here's my example.

345
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:52,200
I wanted to make a photo album.

346
00:16:52,200 --> 00:16:55,680
I let myself get distracted by my old messages.

347
00:16:55,680 --> 00:17:00,800
Then I closed the messages and finished the first page.

348
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:05,080
Mathieu: You can also talk about a usual situation in the present tense.

349
00:17:05,080 --> 00:17:08,400
When I work, notifications distract me.

350
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:11,840
So I turn off the sound for a while.

351
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:16,050
Léa: Choose a version and prepare your first words.

352
00:17:16,100 --> 00:17:18,960
"I wanted" or "When I"?

353
00:17:18,960 --> 00:17:20,640
Mathieu: If you're searching for your words,

354
00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:22,480
you can pause.

355
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:25,760
You don't need to speak as fast as us.

356
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:29,080
Léa: And you can reuse an expression from today.

357
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:32,000
Having your mind elsewhere, losing the thread,

358
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:36,080
putting things off, or getting back to work.

359
00:17:36,080 --> 00:17:38,260
Mathieu: Which one is most like you,

360
00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:38,800
Léa?

361
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:41,520
Léa: Today, getting back to work.

362
00:17:41,520 --> 00:17:44,650
The album finally has a cover.

363
00:17:44,700 --> 00:17:46,840
Mathieu: And your mother, is she happy?

364
00:17:46,840 --> 00:17:48,680
Léa: I haven't given it to her yet.

365
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:51,320
But I chose a photo where she's laughing.

366
00:17:51,320 --> 00:17:53,240
Every time I look at it,

367
00:17:53,240 --> 00:17:55,040
I want to keep going.

368
00:17:55,040 --> 00:17:58,600
Mathieu: That's a lovely reason to come back to your project.

369
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:01,400
Léa: Tell us in the comments what distracts you and

370
00:18:01,400 --> 00:18:03,200
what you'd like to get back to.

371
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:06,540
The pleasure of reading, a real conversation, or

372
00:18:06,540 --> 00:18:08,880
time to learn French.

373
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:12,250
Mathieu: And if you want to keep practising with us,

374
00:18:12,250 --> 00:18:14,530
subscribe to The Cozy French.

375
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:16,960
We'll be happy to see you again.

376
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:19,600
Léa: Thank you for sharing this moment with us.

377
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:22,520
See you very soon for a new conversation.

378
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:23,560
See you soon.

379
00:18:23,560 --> 00:18:27,440
Mathieu: Oh, Léa, can you send me the link to make an album?

380
00:18:27,440 --> 00:18:29,880
Léa: Yes, do you want to make one?

381
00:18:29,880 --> 00:18:34,640
Mathieu: Maybe, but first I need to close the class photo.

382
00:18:34,640 --> 00:18:37,960
I've just found the PE teacher's name.

383
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:39,260
Léa: One investigation at a time,

384
00:18:39,260 --> 00:18:40,840
Mathieu.
